WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.380
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today, Barry Meier, who is the former Pulitzer

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:10.619
Prize winning New York Times reporter and
author of the book Spooked the Secret Rise

00:00:10.619 --> 00:00:12.169
of Private Spies.

00:00:12.169 --> 00:00:14.469
Barry, it's so great to have you on today.

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:16.080
Speaker 5: Thanks, David.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.279
Thanks so much for inviting me on.

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:22.070
Speaker 1: So to start with, when we talk
about private spies, we're not talking about

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:26.839
a private investigator because you suspect
your spouse might be cheating on you.

00:00:26.839 --> 00:00:33.030
We're really talking here about high level
intelligence work that often relates to foreign

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.730
policy, domestic policy, et cetera.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.260
Give us like an overview of this industry
and kind of how it developed.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.970
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:49.780
It really got its start back in the late 1970s
with a gentleman by the name of Jules Kroll,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.950
who created a company called Kroll Associates.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:58.640
And at that time, the public image of the
of, you know, the private investigator was,

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:05.400
as you mentioned, you know, the guy in a raincoat
who, like, hangs around hotels trying to catch

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.820
cheating spouses.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:16.310
Kroll realized that there was a huge marketplace
for investigative services, particularly from

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:21.210
corporations, because this was a period of
time where there were a lot of corporate takeovers

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.590
and shareholder battles.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:29.210
So he formed what was essentially the investigative
equivalent of a law firm.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.190
You know, these guys dressed well, they looked
good.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:41.370
He hired not only FBI agents and former prosecutors,
but really smart college graduates who were

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:49.870
interested in investigations and thus began
this industry that describes itself as, you

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:55.010
know, corporate investigators or corporate
intelligence.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:02:03.660
But basically, they are skilled investigators
who were hired by law firms, Hollywood moguls,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:11.490
political parties, you name it, to go out
and dig up information and often dirt on their

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:12.719
clients adversaries.

00:02:12.719 --> 00:02:18.900
Speaker 1: And this can include I mean, I'm
reading Lawrence Wright's going clear right

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.900
now.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.689
The Church of Scientology employs individuals
like this regularly.

00:02:24.689 --> 00:02:29.879
We could talk about the so-called Trump dossier
and whether that would fit the bill is the

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:34.079
type of product that that would kind of be
produced by this.

00:02:34.079 --> 00:02:38.499
What are some of the maybe less obvious functions
of such private spies?

00:02:38.499 --> 00:02:45.739
Speaker 5: Yeah, so it could be anything from,
you know, what we think of as legitimate use

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:50.139
of investigators, you know, finding missing
people.

00:02:50.139 --> 00:02:57.189
It could be digging up material for lawsuits
and locating witnesses for lawsuits.

00:02:57.189 --> 00:02:59.260
But the range is huge.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:05.180
As you mentioned, groups like Scientology,
which are under public scrutiny, will hire

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:12.340
investigators to dig up information or even
try to intimidate their adversaries or critics.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:17.469
I mean, when I was first working for the top
back when I was working for the Times investigating

00:03:17.469 --> 00:03:24.280
Purdue Pharma, they hired Crowell to investigate
people who were suing the company.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:30.059
And also looked at me not long ago when Patrick
Reed and Keith was writing his book about

00:03:30.059 --> 00:03:37.029
the cyclers, he found investigators sitting
outside his house.

00:03:37.029 --> 00:03:43.849
Then you had cases where Hollywood has, you
know, made use of private investigators all

00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:45.499
the time to intimidate people.

00:03:45.499 --> 00:03:53.019
There was a famous case of Anthony Pellicano
who broke into the car of a Los Angeles Times

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:55.239
reporter who was looking into him.

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:58.670
So, I mean, the gamut is huge.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.870
What kind of unites this industry?

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:09.840
I would say is the willingness of these individuals,
these investigators, private spies, whatever

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:16.639
you want to call them, to use any means necessary
to gather information about their targets.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:22.979
Speaker 1: So that and that gets me to maybe,
you know, we'll talk about in a moment, maybe

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:27.060
some ways in which there's sort of an upside
here to private spies.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.780
But it seems one of the concerns would be
there might be things corporations want done

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:39.360
or governments want done or whoever that they
would be worried about being caught doing.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.190
And the private investigator provides at least
plausible deniability.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:44.190
Is that, in fact, the case?

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.819
Is that what
Speaker 5: exactly?

00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:49.639
You know, it's you know, basically it's private.

00:04:49.639 --> 00:04:54.800
Investigators have been described as the gloves
that lawyers put on when they don't want to

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.949
get their hands dirty.

00:04:56.949 --> 00:04:58.940
And sort of that's that's the concept here.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:03.500
I mean, they will undertake.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.539
Things that, you know, lawyers will claim
they never ordered and are shocked to learn

00:05:08.539 --> 00:05:13.509
when it comes out, they they, in fact, signed
off on it.

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:17.949
Perfect example being David Boys's firm, as
you know, David Boies is probably one of the

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:20.750
best known lawyers in the United States.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:27.300
He's most famous for the Gore Bush recount,
working for the Democratic Party in that case,

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:35.060
also for bringing the litigation in the Supreme
Court that led to the upholding of same sex

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.060
marriage.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:40.240
You know, a good guy in the view in the eyes
of most people.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:50.690
In fact, his law firm signed off on a contract
that called for this.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:58.139
You know, this is really private spying for
Black Cube, who uses kind of very despicable

00:05:58.139 --> 00:06:08.910
types of tactics to one spy on women who were
bringing claims of sexual assault against

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:15.039
Harvey Weinstein and also spy on reporters
for The New Yorker and The New York Times

00:06:15.039 --> 00:06:21.150
who were investigating Harvey Weinstein's
sexual assault allegations.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:27.210
Speaker 1: If people are interested in that,
Ronan Farrow's recent book, the title of the

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:29.500
book Escapes Me, but it's by Ronan Farrow.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.650
And it's about the Harvey Weinstein story.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:37.240
Outlines, outlines that quite a bit to play,
catch and kill.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.240
Yes, exactly.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.439
Now to sort of I don't even know if this is
playing devil's advocate, but to sort of see

00:06:41.439 --> 00:06:46.909
it from the other side, there are definitely
circumstances we could imagine where the power

00:06:46.909 --> 00:06:51.889
of a government, the American government,
for example, could be brought down against

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:58.090
someone for political reasons or for reasons
that you, me or anybody might say are not

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:00.420
on the up and up.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:08.490
Is there a case to be made that the ability
to hire so-called private spy, to try to match,

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:15.120
at least in some small way, the government's
ability to bring political charges and and

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.250
use their investigative power against an individual
or a corporation could be a good thing.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:20.939
I mean, I'm thinking out loud here.

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:27.370
Speaker 5: Yes, I mean, you know, let me give
you a perfect example, because there's a lawsuit

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:35.210
